<!doctype html>
<html lang="<%= locale %>" data-release="<%= locals.releaseId || 'dev' %>">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0, viewport-fit=cover" />
  <title><%= title %></title>

  <!-- keeps the browser chrome on the same palette as the page -->
  <meta name="theme-color" content="#fafafc" media="(prefers-color-scheme: light)" />
  <meta name="theme-color" content="#0a0a11" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" />

  <!-- favicon -->
  <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/assets/images/icon/favicon.svg">
  <link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="32x32" href="/assets/images/icon/favicon-32x32.png">
  <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" sizes="any">
  <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/assets/images/icon/apple-touch-icon.png">
  <%# No `rel="manifest"` here, unlike the other two layouts. A tailnet-only
      desk tool is not something anyone installs to a home screen, and the
      standalone display mode would take the browser chrome away from a surface
      whose operators rely on it.

      The tailnet listener does match /site.webmanifest all the same — see the
      note on that matcher in Caddyfile — because layouts/plain renders
      /admin/login and does carry the link. -%>

  <%#
    EVERY ASSET BELOW HAS TO SIT UNDER A PREFIX THE TAILNET LISTENER MATCHES.

    `/admin` answers only on the :8080 listener, whose matcher is pinned in
    Caddyfile:66 — /admin, /admin/*, /api/v1/admin*, /css/*, /js/*, /assets/*
    and /favicon.ico. An admin asset under any other prefix (/vendor/*,
    /fonts/*) is served fine in development, where the request never reaches
    that listener, and 404s over the tailnet where the surface actually runs.

    Adding one means extending that matcher in the same change.
  -%>
  <!-- global styles -->
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/dist/main.css" />

  <!-- stored theme, applied before first paint. The toggle lives in the rail
       rather than the public footer, but the stored choice is the same one. -->
  <%- include('../partials/theme-boot') %>

  <!-- the shell's own styles, linked once here so each screen adds only its own -->
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/dist/pages/admin.css" />

  <!-- page-specific styles -->
  <%- defineContent('styles') %>
</head>
<%#
  The administrator shell.

  A third layout beside main.ejs and plain.ejs rather than a reuse of either.
  plain.ejs wraps its body in .plain-wrapper — 46rem, sized for long-form
  reading — and carries the public footer's privacy and terms links, which are
  attendee-facing. main.ejs paints a full-viewport boot screen that only
  JavaScript removes and hides <main> under <noscript>, both wrong for a desk
  tool reached over a tailnet.

  Deliberately absent for the same reason: the boot screen, and any <noscript>
  block that hides content. The rail is built to work without JavaScript;
  hiding <main> would take that away.

  The Cloudflare challenge gate used to be on that list, on the grounds that
  /admin is tailnet-only and a challenge cannot reach it. It is carried now:
  this surface answers on the public hostname behind Cloudflare Access
  (docs/infrastructure/cloudflare-rules.md), and the gate is what a lapsed cf_clearance meets.
  Without it raiseChallengeGate() logs `no gate on this layout` and the
  volunteer sees nothing at all.

  `data-nodeenv` is what client/modules/log/logger.ts reads to pick its
  threshold. plain.ejs never rendered it, so every debug and info line on the
  admin surface was dropped even in development.
-%>
<body class="admin-page" data-nodeenv="<%= nodeEnv %>">

  <a class="admin-skip" href="#conteudo"><%= shell.skipLink() %></a>

  <%# First in the DOM on purpose: with JavaScript off this renders as a plain
      stacked list above the content and every link still works. The drawer is
      an enhancement, not a requirement. -%>
  <%- include('../partials/admin-nav') %>

  <main class="admin-main" id="conteudo">
    <%- body %>
  </main>

  <%# Here rather than per screen: every editor that saves data will want
      modules/ui/toast.ts, and it needs this markup to write into. -%>
  <%- include('../components/toast') %>

  <%# edge-challenge recovery. The blocked copy is overridden because the
      shared string tells the reader to call a volunteer, and here the reader
      is one. -%>
  <%- include('../components/challenge-gate', {
    challengeBlocked: chrome.challengeBlockedAdmin(),
  }) %>

  <!-- global scripts -->
  <script type="module" src="/js/theme/theme.js"></script>
  <%# Installs window.onerror and unhandledrejection. Loaded on every page
      of both surfaces rather than imported by one, which is why it is its
      own bundle entry — see ENTRY_PATTERNS in scripts/build-client.ts. -%>
  <script type="module" src="/js/telemetry/index.js"></script>
  <script type="module" src="/js/pages/admin/shell/index.js"></script>

  <!-- page-specific scripts -->
  <%- defineContent('scripts') %>
</body>
</html>
